  "image_name": "0ecb3a31957bbfb9a19046445384979c.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates how a distant enhancer can activate transcription at a promoter through chromatin looping and a large bridging complex. The DNA is shown as a blue strand wrapped around nucleosomes, emphasizing that the regulatory elements are separated along the chromosome but brought close together in three-dimensional space. A cohesin ring appears to stabilize the loop, while an enhancer-bound set of transcription factors connects to the promoter through the Mediator complex, specifically labeled with a CDK8-associated form.\n\nAt the promoter, general transcription factors and RNA polymerase II assemble into the transcription initiation machinery. The enhancer region also seems to recruit RNA polymerase II and produce a short noncoding RNA, suggesting enhancer transcription alongside promoter activation. The overall message is that gene regulation depends not just on linear DNA sequence but on physical contacts between enhancer and promoter, mediated by chromatin architecture and protein complexes.\n\nVisually, the figure highlights a coordinated mechanism: enhancer-bound factors, Mediator, cohesin, and promoter-bound initiation factors work together to position Pol II and stimulate RNA synthesis. Some fine details of the intermediate factors are small, but the central idea of long-range enhancer–promoter communication is clear.",
